/*****************************************************************************
    parseCode -- kant parse methods, one entry per rule.

    GENERATED BY hand, from the term list, 2026-08-12. No emitter exists.
    ASOF the SEQ 55 seal build of 2026-08-11 plus the full monty rung.
    DENOMINATOR one rule of the forty seven that can consume an install bind.

    THE ENTRY BELOW IS Braced. Its term list in the grammar is a bracket
    literal, then a reference to ExpressioN, then a closing bracket literal.
    The body names each term by position and holds no node, which is the
    standing convention. There is no parseTerms declaration -- Tony struck it,
    and the bounds are checked instead by kantDoor, which reads the highest
    position the body names and refuses past the live term count.

    THE TAIL. actK runs the rule's C++ action. It takes no argument because
    the frame supplies the label and the rule, exactly as it supplies position
    and mark to litK and parseRK. Both direct call spellings were measured
    unsound on 2026-08-12, in docs/parseCodeMeasurements.md addendum d, so the
    shim exists because of a measurement rather than for tidiness.

    ONE FIRE SITE BY CONSTRUCTION. kantDoor clears the rule's method slot when
    it installs, so the fire inside parse does nothing and the tail is the only
    route to the action.

    ⚠ THE define WRAPPER IS REQUIRED, AND THE DESIGN SAID IT WAS NOT. Measured
    2026-08-12. A bare top level entry is read as a RunRulE invocation and
    rejected with `RunRulE expected a method not code`, which abandons the rest
    of the file and still exits zero. The M2 fixture cited as verifying the
    bare form did carry a define wrapper. It verified merging from outside the
    REGISTRY, which is a different claim. The file is still definitions only.
    define is the definition command, not a registry.

    ⚠ READ docs/kantParseTemplates.md BEFORE EDITING. It carries the pick
    rationale, the term kind table, and the three outcome pre registration this
    entry must be read against.

    =========================================================================
    ⚠ REVISED 2026-08-13, SEQ 63, AND TWO THINGS ABOVE ARE NOW HISTORY. The
    entry was written for the MINT door, which is still unbuilt. Today it is
    bound through the dlsym door, parseMethod=parseViaKant, which is the door
    SEQ 58 repaired and proved. Two consequences, both mechanism and neither
    taste.

    THE ACTION IS NAMED kpBraced, NOT Braced. parseViaKant builds the name it
    wants as kp then the rule tag and locates THAT. The mint route reaches the
    same place by minting kp<Tag> and hanging the body on it, so under the mint
    the file could name the rule; under the dlsym door it must name the action.
    An entry named Braced is simply not found, and parseViaKant refuses loudly.

    AND NAMING THE RULE WOULD NOW DO SOMETHING ELSE AS WELL. Since SEQ 56 the
    coded test wins the dispatch in aCTionDefinE, so a body attached to the
    rule Braced sets its method to processAction. Naming the action sidesteps
    that entirely; the rule keeps its own C++ action untouched.

    THE actK TAIL IS GONE, and the header paragraph above explains why it has
    to be. actK exists because kantDoor CLEARS the rule method slot when it
    installs, making the tail the only route to the action. No kantDoor runs
    here, so the slot is intact and parse fires the action itself -- keeping
    the tail would fire it TWICE. The tail returns when its door does.
    =========================================================================
*****************************************************************************/

define
    kpBraced code={
        return litK(1) AND parseRK(2) AND litK(3);
        };
    ;
